### 3.4 Results

In this section the \(\pi^{0}\) spectra and the nuclear modification factors \(R_{AA}\) and \(R_{CP}\) in 200 GeV Au+Au collisions are presented. They are compared to earlier STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\) and PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\) results, as well as the theoretical calculation of light quark energy loss through gluon radiation.

#### STAR \(\pi^{0}\) spectra

Figure 3.33 shows the \(\pi^{0}\) invariant yield per event at mid-rapidity (\(0<\eta<0.8\)) as a function of \(p_{T}\) in 200 GeV Au+Au collisions for different centrality bins. Statistical and systematic errors are shown as vertical lines and bars respectively. The \(\pi^{0}\) spectra are measured over an extended \(p_{T}\) range from 1 to 12 GeV/\(c\). For central triggered events the spectrum is measured up to 5 GeV/\(c\) due to the lack of HT triggered events. Results from different \(\pi^{0}\) reconstruction algorithms and different data samples are compared in overlapping \(p_{T}\) range and agree well. Therefore the combined averaged result will be used in the following figures.

The STAR \(\pi^{0}\) results are compared to earlier STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\) and PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\) results. Due to different \(p_{T}\) bin definitions, a Levy-function fit of earlier results is used. The ratios of spectra over their fits are shown in Figure 3.34, which indicate that the fit describes the data well.

The ratios of the STAR \(\pi^{0}\) spectra over the STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\)[71] and the PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\)[16] in 200 GeV Au+Au collisions are shown in Figure 3.35. The error bars of earlier results are propagated using the averaged error of two neighboring data points. The spectrum ratio is larger in peripheral collisions than in central and mid-central collisions, where the yield of STAR \(\pi^{0}\) appears to be slightly lower than the STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\) but closer to the PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\). With the best statistics in MB 0-80% centrality, the STAR \(\pi^{0}\) yields are about 15% smaller than the STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\) over the \(p_{T}\) range, but they are still consistent within systematic uncertainties. The discrepancy has not yet been fully understood. One possible reason may be an overall offset of the BEMC energy scale, indicated by the \(p_{T}\) independent ratio distribution. It has been demonstrated in the systematic uncertainty study that the \(\pi^{0}\) spectra are very sensitive to it. However,Figure 3.33: Invariant yield distribution of STAR \(\pi^{0}\) as a function of \(p_{T}\) at mid-rapidity for different centrality bins in 200 GeV Au+Au collisions. Spectra for different centralities are scaled for clarity. Statistical errors are shown as vertical lines and point to point systematic errors are shown as bars.

one caveat of this possibility is that it might contradict the results shown in Figure 3.11. Figure 3.11 shows that the reconstructed \(\pi^{0}\) peak position from embedding is 5-10 MeV/\(c^{2}\) lower than real data, which can indicate that the BEMC energy scale in data is too high. However, from Figure 3.30 of the energy scale study, increasing energy scale in embedding analysis to compensate the offset results in a decrease of the measured \(\pi^{0}\) spectra, and therefore, would result in a larger difference between STAR \(\pi^{0}\) and earlier \(\pi\) results.

