#### Future analysis with run7 data

Au+Au 200 GeV data produced in 2007 RHIC run (run 7) will provide significantly more statistics for the \(\pi^{0}\) production analysis. The advantages of run7 data include a higher HT trigger threshold of \(E_{T}>5.5\) GeV, a full BEMC coverage of \(|\eta|<1\), and much larger data sample of 605.7 \(\mu b^{-1}\) rare triggered events and \(75.8\times 10^{6}\) MB-VPD triggered events. So using run7 data can measure the \(\pi^{0}\) production with higher precision and extend the spectra to higher \(p_{T}\). A preliminary study of the run7 express stream data reveals that we can obtain a clear \(\pi^{0}\) signal at as high as 12 GeV/\(c\) for the EMC-TPC method, and 15 GeV/\(c\) for the EMC-EMC method, which are shown in Figure 3.38.

#### Summary

We have presented the STAR results for the \(\pi^{0}\) production in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)=200 GeV. The \(\pi^{0}\) spectra are measured over the range of 1\(<p_{T}<\)12 GeV/\(c\) using the combination of TPC conversion photons and BEMC photons. Despite the relatively large tower size, the STAR BEMC itself can reliably identify \(\pi^{0}\) at high \(p_{T}\) above 4 GeV/\(c\). The use of conversion photons significantly improves the \(\pi^{0}\) invariant mass signal at low and intermediate \(p_{T}\) and extends our study to a wide \(p_{T}\) range.

Compared to the STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\) and PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\), the overall STAR \(\pi^{0}\) spectra are consistent with PHENIX \(\pi^{0}\), and about 15% lower than STAR \(\pi^{\pm}\), but still consistent within systematic errors. The nuclear modification factors \(R_{CP}\) and \(R_{AA}\) of the STAR \(\pi^{0}\) are consistent with the earlier \(\pi\) results and the theoretical calculation based on jet quenching. At the most central Au+Au collisions the inclusive \(\pi^{0}\) yield shows a factor of 5 suppression relative to the p+p collisions at high \(p_{T}\). This result confirms the suppression of light mesons and provides additional evidence for the jet quenching picture.

